From: Kreijstal <elektrischrainbow@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 05:09:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] pbd/test: skip the reader-preference RWLock test on
 Windows

RWLockTest::run_thread_sequence_test checks that a second read-lock can
be acquired while a writer is blocked waiting for the first reader.
That holds for glibc's default (reader-preferring) pthread rwlock, but
std::shared_mutex makes no such promise. On MinGW it is backed by
winpthreads, whose rwlock blocks new readers as soon as a writer
waits, so the try-lock fails and CPPUNIT_ASSERT in the worker thread
terminates the whole test runner.

Only register the test on non-Windows platforms, documenting why.
---
 libs/pbd/test/rwlock_test.h | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libs/pbd/test/rwlock_test.h b/libs/pbd/test/rwlock_test.h
index ff2b90a..b319a6e 100644
--- a/libs/pbd/test/rwlock_test.h
+++ b/libs/pbd/test/rwlock_test.h
@@ -9,7 +9,13 @@ class RWLockTest : public CppUnit::TestFixture
 	CPPUNIT_TEST_SUITE (RWLockTest);
 	CPPUNIT_TEST (single_thread_test);
 	CPPUNIT_TEST (run_thread_test);
+#ifndef PLATFORM_WINDOWS
+	/* Taking another read-lock while a writer is waiting is not
+	 * possible with winpthreads' (writer-preferring) rwlock, which backs
+	 * std::shared_mutex on MinGW, nor with Windows SRW locks.
+	 */
 	CPPUNIT_TEST (run_thread_sequence_test);
+#endif
 	CPPUNIT_TEST_SUITE_END ();
 
 public:
